# Per-chromosome drift: panel-coverage attribution

**Attribution.** A controlled per-probe ΔLAI experiment on 300 AoU
individuals (60 per superpopulation) attributes the per-chromosome step
to reference-panel coverage geometry, not cohort composition. Holding
the panel fixed and varying cohort composition (mu_afr from 0.076 to
0.679 across three cohorts) produces no per-sample chr2 ΔLAI on the same
individuals. Holding the cohort fixed and relabeling half the afr panel
donors away from afr (no haplotype changes, labels only) moves the same
individuals' chr2 afr posterior by a median of 0.468. The figure below
shows the corroborating signal on the unmodified production cohort:
per-chromosome FLARE-minus-Rye residuals, faceted by Rye-dominant
ancestry. The afr facet has a wider residual spread than any other on
every chromosome and a negative median throughout; the eur facet has a
positive median throughout with roughly one-fifth the spread of afr. The
convergent reading across the two directions of the residual is that
FLARE leaks afr-mass onto neighboring panels at apply time, an effect
the panel experiment makes acute by relabeling. This validation cycle
documents the drift but applies no remediation; the panel-augmentation
workstream is tracked separately.

